<section class="page" id="foreword">
  <h2 class="page-title">FOREWORD</h2>
  <div class="panel bg-blue halftone" style="font-size:17px;line-height:1.6">
    <p>At the southern tip of India, on a low island between the Bay of Bengal and the Gulf of Mannar, Rāma himself is said to have worshipped Śiva. That is what makes Rāmeśvaram unique among the twelve: the jyotirliṅga installed by an avatāra of Viṣṇu. Śaivas and Vaiṣṇavas alike come here, and it is both a jyotirliṅga and one of the four <b>dhāms</b> of India.</p>
    <p>Its temple is a world of its own: pillared corridors that run for hundreds of metres, built by the Setupati kings of Rāmanāthapuram; 22 sacred wells whose water is poured over every pilgrim; a liṅga of sand shaped by Sītā; and a liṅga from Kailāsa brought by Hanumān.</p>
    <p>This eighth guide in our series follows the seventh name in the stotram: its legends, its long history, its rituals, and the old bond that ties Rāmeśvaram to Kāśī at the other end of India.</p>
    <p>May every reader one day walk the great corridor at dawn, wet from the 22 wells, toward the Lord whom Rāma worshipped.</p>
    <p style="text-align:right;margin-top:14px"><span class="bang" style="font-size:22px;color:#1d4f9a">Rāmanātha śaraṇam,</span><br><span class="bang" style="font-size:26px">srini.presents</span> 🙏</p>
  </div>
</section>

